    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Measuring text complexity is a crucial aspect of how we define and operationalize reading levels and metrics in K-12 curriculums and beyond. Applying large language models (LLMs) to measure readability and complexity offers a new opportunity over rigid, formulaic metrics. This project explores the effectiveness of a Robustly Optimized BERT Pretraining Approach (RoBERTa) language model in assessing the readability of textual passages in comparison to human-rated scores. Utilizing the CLEAR Corpus dataset, comprising various passages annotated with readability scores by teachers, we fine-tune the RoBERTa model to predict these scores, aiming to understand the nuances\r\nof text complexity as interpreted by machine learning. Through quantitative metrics such as Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) and Pearson Correlation, we evaluate the model’s predictive performance and understand how accurately the model can extract the features of readability. The findings reveal an improvement of up to 25% using LLMs in automated readability assessment tools, highlighting the model’s capacity and accuracy.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Garg, A. (2024). Using LLMs To Improve Formulaic Readability Measures: A RoBERTa Approach. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/nq298nj9707. https://doi.org/10.25740/nq298nj9707.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
